  "body": "## Description:\nThe HypoVault contract initializes 1,000,000 bootstrap shares in its constructor that belong to no user but participate in profit distribution calculations. These \"virtual\" shares are included in the totalSupply used for share-to-asset conversion ratios during withdrawals, causing them to implicitly claim a portion of vault profits despite not representing any actual user deposits.\n\n```solidity\nconstructor(...) {\n    // ...\n    totalSupply = 1_000_000;  // Bootstrap shares that belong to no one\n}\n```\n\nThe share-to-asset conversion in `fulfillWithdrawals` includes these bootstrap shares:\n\n```solidity\nfunction fulfillWithdrawals(...) external onlyManager {\n    uint256 _totalSupply = totalSupply;  // Includes bootstrap shares\n    uint256 assetsReceived = Math.mulDiv(sharesToFulfill, totalAssets, _totalSupply);\n    // Bootstrap shares dilute the conversion rate\n}\n```\n\n## Attack path:\n1. HypoVault is deployed with 1,000,000 bootstrap shares in totalSupply\n2. Users deposit funds and receive legitimate shares proportional to their deposits\n3. Vault generates profits through manager operations\n4. When users withdraw, the conversion rate calculation includes bootstrap shares in totalSupply\n5. Bootstrap shares effectively \"claim\" their proportional share of vault profits\n6. User returns are diluted by the percentage that bootstrap shares represent of total supply\n\n## Impact:\nUsers suffer permanent loss of yield proportional to bootstrap shares' percentage of total supply\n\n## Recommended Mitigation:\n\nModify withdrawal logic to calculate conversion rates using only user-owned shares, excluding the initial bootstrap amount. The virtual shares should not earn interest as they don't correspond to any supplier.",
